> > I also see an indication that if you call gcc with the -mieee option, it
> > will generate code that runs on any alpha correctly, without relying on
> > the kernel to solve the problem for it.
>
> Your conclusion seems wrong since the test is built with "-mieee
> -mfp-rounding-mode=3Dd -frounding-math".

As far as my understanding goes, this is not how the kernel module and -mie=
ee=20
work.  I understand the difference in code generated with -mieee is that th=
e=20
ASM is produced in such a way that precise exceptions handling can be done.

Precise exception handling is necessary for the kernel to be able to restar=
t=20
code after an exception/trap occurs.  Without it, the kernel has to kill an=
y=20
program once a math fixup trap occurs (as it will when any such things as=20
infs, nans, etc., that are not supported in hardware, are encountered).

The difference in having the module load/not loaded is when precise excepti=
on=20
handling is in effect (i.e., -mieee) and a trap occurs.  Without the module=
=20
the kernel just zeros the result and continues.  With the module it generat=
es=20
the proper IEEE result and continues.

Note this does not apply to anything after the EV56 as they have both (I=20
believe it is both, but it may also be just one or the other) precise=20
exceptions in all cases and hardware support for full IEEE specification.

Later!  -Tyson

PS:  The debian default is -mieee on unless --ffinte-math-only (part=20
of -ffast-math) is specified (this was not the original default and getting=
=20
it this way was the solution to a lot of pain in the form of random SIGFPEs=
).

